**`app/services/backup_providers/ftp_provider.py:52-77`** — `upload()` and `download()` call `ftp.quit()` only on the success path:

```python
def upload(self, filename: str, data: bytes) -> ProviderResult:
    try:
        ftp = self._connect()
        ...
        ftp.storbinary(f"STOR {filename}", io.BytesIO(data))
        ftp.quit()                              # only reached on success
        return ProviderResult(True, ...)
    except Exception as ex:
        return ProviderResult(False, ...)       # ftp socket never closed here
```

Since `auto_backup()` is triggered on every check-in, checkout, booking, and monthly-rental action (confirmed via the `_auto_backup(db, trigger=...)` calls in `rooms.py`), any transient FTP failure — timeout, permission error, disk full on the remote — leaks one open TCP socket per failed attempt in this long-running desktop process. Repeated failures (e.g., misconfigured credentials left in place) will slowly exhaust sockets. Fix with `try/finally`:

```python
def upload(self, filename: str, data: bytes) -> ProviderResult:
    ftp = None
    try:
        ftp = self._connect()
        path = self._remote_path()
        try:
            ftp.cwd(path)
        except ftplib.error_perm:
            ftp.mkd(path)
            ftp.cwd(path)
        ftp.storbinary(f"STOR {filename}", io.BytesIO(data))
        return ProviderResult(True, f"✅ 上傳成功：{filename}")
    except Exception as ex:
        return ProviderResult(False, f"❌ 上傳失敗：{ex}")
    finally:
        if ftp is not None:
            try:
                ftp.quit()
            except Exception:
                ftp.close()   # force-close if quit() itself fails (e.g. dead socket)
```

Apply the same pattern to `download()` and to `_connect()` failures inside `test_connection()` (if `login()` throws after `connect()` succeeds, the socket that was opened inside `_connect()` is abandoned with no reference to close it).

